  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1123 AM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nAbove normal temperatures and dry conditions will persist through\nmidweek underneath a ridge of high pressure. Diurnal temperature\nranges will vary widely, featuring lows in the upper 40s and\nlower 50s with highs in the mid 70s to low 80s. This air mass is\nanomalously dry for late October, housing precipitable water\nvalues around the 25th percentile. This dry air will keep\nrelative humidity values around 30 percent through midweek, which\nwhen combined with dry fuels will result in elevated fire weather\nconcerns. Several grass fires have been reported across the Mid-\nSouth over the last several days. Ensure you are following local\nburn bans and disposing of cigarettes properly.\n\nBy midweek, the upper level ridge will begin to deamplify as a\nseries of shortwaves pass northwest of the forecast area. With\nthe ridge out of the way, a subtropical jet will have no issue\nmaneuvering over the Mid-South late Friday into Saturday. This\nfeature will bring our first areawide rain chances since the\nremnants of Hurricane Isaias impacted the region in early\nOctober. Unfortunately, the latest guidance is increasing rain\nprobabilities on Halloween with rain chances continuing into\nSunday. The LREF has a 20 percent chance of more than 1 inch of\nrain falling across the area this weekend. While not much,\nanything will be beneficial.\n\nLong-term guidance keeps an active synoptic pattern next week.\nBoth the GFS and ECMWF depict a large trough over CONUS. However,\nprecise placement and timing of this feature varies. For now, we\ncan expect rain chances to linger into the first week of\nNovember.",
